) on a street of a given width: 14 ft on streets 66 to 80 ft wide, 10 ft on streets 50 to 60 ft wide (sec. 2072)","Where a walk stands inside that space: one foot from and parallel with the lot line, unless a special ordinance says otherwise (sec. 2062)","The walk widths the city's paving specification anticipates (5, 6, 10, 12, 15, 18, 20, 24 and 25 ft; sec. 2062), which bound a reconstructed walk width","That the rest of the sidewalk space could be a planted court or grass plat (sec. 2077), i.e. a parkway","The curb's minimum thickness where one was required (3 in, stone or white oak; sec. 2072) and the cross-fall of the sidewalk space (1 in in 3 ft toward the curb; the 'Slope' sections, pp. 552-553)","The period's permitted MATERIALS, as bounds (T-1728): walks of Portland cement concrete in 5 x 6 ft blocks, of limestone, or of cinders between wooden curbs, and no new wooden walk (secs. 2062, 2068, 2070, 2079); curbing of stone or white-oak plank (sec. 2072) or concrete with a concrete walk (sec. 2066); roadways of granite, asphalt, macadam, brick, cedar block or creosoted block (sec. 1944)"]},"uses":["other_scene"]}
